<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <!-- `deploy/Dockerfile.collector` runs this by name, and the name is the
         one in the `docker run` line an operator is handed. -->
    <AssemblyName>logaffe-collector</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Logaffe.Collector</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Everything here is internal: this is an executable and nothing consumes
       it as a library, so a public type would be a claim about an audience that
       does not exist. The unit project tests the parsing, which is where a
       reading of /proc can be wrong. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Logaffe.UnitTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- No project references and no packages, and both are the point.

       It references none of the four layers because it is not part of them
       (`docs/codebase.md`): it reads a machine and posts numbers over HTTP, the
       same relationship to the installation a sending application has, so what
       it depends on is the wire format and nothing else.

       It carries no packages because it runs on every machine the operator
       wants numbers from, which makes its dependency list a surface on
       somebody else's hosts rather than only on the installation's. HTTP and
       JSON are in the framework; a logger that writes a line to standard output
       is nine lines here and a package everywhere else. ADR 0043 asks for this
       to be small enough to be boring, and this is what that costs and buys. -->

</Project>
